# ReelMakeEasy configuration. Copy to .env (or run `pnpm reelmakeeasy setup`).
# Required: FAL_KEY or GEMINI_API_KEY. Either one makes complete videos.
# Writing and planning run on Codex or Claude Code signed in on this computer,
# or on Gemini when its key is set. Everything else here is optional.
# Keys are read when ReelMakeEasy starts: restart `pnpm reelmakeeasy start` (or
# `pnpm dev`) after editing them.

# ─── Video keys (one or both) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# fal: pictures, clips, music and voices. Without ELEVENLABS_API_KEY, ElevenLabs
# voices run through fal too, and so does caption timing when it isn't done on
# this computer. https://fal.ai/dashboard/keys
FAL_KEY=

# Gemini: pictures (Nano Banana), clips (Veo 3.1), voices, caption timing and
# music (Lyria), and it can write and plan as well. https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ─── ElevenLabs (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ElevenLabs voices, the voice checks (speaker labels and sounds), ElevenLabs
# voice cloning (which needs it), and caption timing when it isn't done on this
# computer, all on your own key. https://elevenlabs.io/app/settings/api-keys
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# ─── Who does what (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pick these on the app's Setup page (sidebar); a choice saved there wins over
# these defaults. Unset, ReelMakeEasy uses the first one that is ready.
# Which AI writes and plans: claude | codex | gemini | endpoint
# (first ready of claude, codex, gemini, endpoint; AGENT_HARNESS breaks a
# claude/codex tie).
# LLM_PROVIDER=claude
# Who makes the pictures, clips and voice when both video keys are set: fal | google
# ("gemini" works too)
# GENERATION_PROVIDER=fal

# ─── Chat endpoint (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Write and plan on any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (OpenRouter, OpenAI, Ollama,
# vLLM, ...): set these, then pick "endpoint" in Setup. LLM_API_KEY is sent as
# the bearer token.
# LLM_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# LLM_API_KEY=
# Models used for each tier; requested model ids are mapped onto these.
# Same as CHAT_<TIER>_MODEL (see "Models" below), which wins if both are set.
# LLM_MODEL_LITE=gemini-3.1-flash-lite
# LLM_MODEL_FLASH=gemini-3.5-flash
# LLM_MODEL_PRO=gemini-3.8-flash
# Send requested model ids to a custom endpoint as-is instead of mapping to tiers.
# LLM_PASSTHROUGH=1

# ─── Models (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# reelmakeeasy.models.json (repo root) maps each role to a model; it holds no
# keys. reelmakeeasy.models.example.json shows alternatives. Restart the API after
# changing the file or any of these variables.
# MODELS_FILE=./my.models.json
#
# Override one role with <ROLE>_MODEL: the role name upper-cased
# with "." as "_". Values are a registry key, an alias, or any fal endpoint id.
# A Gemini model (the *-google keys) changes the role used when Gemini makes the
# pictures and clips; any other value changes the fal role.
# Roles: CHAT_LITE CHAT_FLASH CHAT_PRO IMAGE_GENERATE IMAGE_EDIT IMAGE_FAST
# VIDEO_TEXT VIDEO_IMAGE VIDEO_FIRSTLAST VIDEO_REFERENCE VIDEO_LIPSYNC MUSIC TTS
# TTS_ELEVENLABS TTS_MINIMAX TTS_KOKORO TTS_QWEN3 VOICE_CLONE SPEECH_ALIGN
# SPEECH_TRANSCRIBE TTS_GEMINI
# VIDEO_FIRSTLAST_MODEL=veo-3.1-fast
# On Gemini, clips default to Omni Flash (omni-flash-google), made at the length
# each clip needs. Veo on the Gemini API takes a last frame only on 8 s clips,
# so a Veo clip is sped up and cut to fit; pick one in the clip menu, or:
# VIDEO_FIRSTLAST_MODEL=veo-3.1-lite-google   # Gemini: the cheapest Veo
#
# Group overrides (a role variable wins over these):
# IMAGE_MODEL=nano-banana-pro   # image.generate, image.edit, image.fast
# VIDEO_MODEL=veo-3.1-fast      # video.text, video.image, video.firstLast
# MUSIC_MODEL=lyria2            # music

# ─── Free local voices ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `pnpm reelmakeeasy setup` installs Kokoro and Qwen3-TTS to run on this machine
# (Apple Silicon via MLX, NVIDIA via CUDA, Kokoro only on CPU), and
# `pnpm reelmakeeasy start` (or `pnpm dev`) starts them. Those voices are then
# free; without them they run on fal (paid).
# Models download from public Hugging Face repos: no account or token needed.
# Skip with `pnpm reelmakeeasy setup --local-tts=off`, or turn off here:
# LOCAL_TTS=off
# Port of the local voice server (start and dev pass LOCAL_TTS_URL to the API).
# LOCAL_TTS_PORT=5019
# Force a backend instead of detecting one: mlx | cuda | mps | cpu
# LOCAL_TTS_DEVICE=auto
# Point the API at a voice server you run yourself (e.g. on a GPU box).
# LOCAL_TTS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5019
# Qwen3 runs locally only with enough memory (tools/local-tts/hardware.json):
# 24 GB RAM on Apple Silicon (36 GB recommended), or 8 GB GPU memory and 32 GB
# RAM on NVIDIA (Windows, Linux). Otherwise Kokoro runs locally and Qwen3 voices use fal.
# Qwen3 models kept loaded at once (default 2, or 1 below the recommended RAM).
# LOCAL_TTS_MAX_MODELS=2
# Unload models after this many idle seconds (0 keeps them loaded).
# LOCAL_TTS_IDLE_UNLOAD_SECONDS=300
# Apple Silicon: memory MLX may keep cached for reuse, in GB.
# LOCAL_TTS_MLX_CACHE_GB=1
# Download models through a Hugging Face mirror (e.g. https://hf-mirror.com).
# HF_ENDPOINT=

# ─── Free local caption timing ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Setup also installs Whisper and a forced aligner (about 2 GB; 0.9 GB on a CPU),
# even with --local-tts=off, and the local server above times captions with them.
# Without them, ElevenLabs, fal or Gemini time the captions (paid).
# Skip with `pnpm reelmakeeasy setup --local-captions=off`, or turn off here:
# LOCAL_ASR=off
# Whisper model (Hugging Face repo) instead of the one picked for this backend.
# Run `pnpm reelmakeeasy setup` after changing it: start never downloads models.
# LOCAL_ASR_MODEL=
# Forced aligner model (Hugging Face repo) instead of the one for this backend.
# Run `pnpm reelmakeeasy setup` after changing it too.
# LOCAL_ALIGN_MODEL=

# ─── Voice typing (the microphone in the chat boxes) ─────────────────────────
# Which engine turns speech into text; the Setup page can change it. auto (the
# default) picks Gemini, then Whisper above on Apple Silicon or NVIDIA, then fal
# (ElevenLabs Scribe), then the browser's own speech recognition. One of:
# auto, gemini, local, fal, browser, off.
# DICTATION=auto
# The Gemini model voice typing uses.
# DICTATION_GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-transcribe
# smart drops "um"s and adds punctuation; verbatim keeps every word.
# DICTATION_GEMINI_MODE=smart

# ─── Infrastructure ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave empty and `pnpm reelmakeeasy start` (or `pnpm dev`) starts an embedded MongoDB.
# MongoDB must run as a replica set (Prisma requirement).
# DATABASE_URL=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/reelmakeeasy?replicaSet=rs0&directConnection=true

# Where projects, media and the embedded database are stored.
# DATA_DIR=.reelmakeeasy
# Scratch folder for joining long renders. Defaults to the system temp folder.
# APP_TMP_DIR=
# How many renders run at once. They are CPU heavy; raise it on larger machines.
# RENDER_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=1

# API port. Another PORT points the web app at this API, so a second copy can
# run beside the first (give it another WEB_PORT too).
# PORT=5017
# Web app port.
# WEB_PORT=3000
# Where the API and the web app listen. The default, 127.0.0.1, answers this
# computer only; 0.0.0.0 answers every network interface. ReelMakeEasy has no
# sign-in, so only do that behind a firewall or an authenticating proxy.
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# The URL browsers use to load stored media.
# PUBLIC_FILES_URL=http://localhost:5017/api/files

# Shared read-only assets (caption fonts, effect templates, style thumbnails).
# PUBLIC_ASSETS_URL=

# Install the preset pack (style thumbnails and previews) from a local archive
# instead of downloading it. See presets/README.md.
# PRESETS_PACK=./reelmakeeasy-presets-<version>.tar.gz

# The editor's preview engine loads from the preset pack by default. Set a URL
# to load another build (a gzipped AVEditorEngine.wasm).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WASM_PATH=

# ─── Access ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ReelMakeEasy has no sign-in and no access control: anyone who can reach the API
# acts as the single local user. It listens on 127.0.0.1 unless HOST (above)
# says otherwise; beyond this computer, keep it behind a firewall or an
# authenticating reverse proxy.
# Key that signs upload URLs. Unset, a random one is generated into the data
# folder on first run.
# FILE_SIGNING_SECRET=

# ─── Agent mode (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Agent chats run on your own Codex or Claude Code (picked in each chat's mode
# card). This is the one the card preselects: codex or claude. Default codex.
# It also decides which writes and plans when both are signed in and
# LLM_PROVIDER is unset.
# AGENT_HARNESS=codex
# ReelMakeEasy finds Codex automatically: PATH, Codex.app, or the ChatGPT desktop
# app's bundled copy; set this only to point at another binary.
# CODEX_BIN=
# Claude Code: found on PATH (or ~/.claude/local, ~/.local/bin); set this only to
# point at another binary. Sign in once with `claude login`.
# CLAUDE_BIN=
# The model Claude chats and their video planning run on.
# CLAUDE_AGENT_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# Optional: bill Claude Code agent chats to an Anthropic API key instead of your
# Claude Code login.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ─── Reference image search (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Google Programmable Search for reference images, both comma-separated: API
# keys, and the search engine ids (cx) to query. Empty skips the search.
GOOGLE_SEARCH_API_KEYS=
GOOGLE_SEARCH_ENGINE_IDS=
